\subsection{Automated Stability Enforcement}
When stress conditions approach the boundary of the safe operating region
$\partial\mathcal{R}_{\text{safe}}$, the system activates kernel-level
stability mechanisms:

\paragraph{Adaptive Damping.}
If local potential energy $U(p)$ approaches rupture limits, the damping
operator $\mathbf{D}(p)$ increases viscosity to satisfy the sector condition
\begin{equation}
\langle \mathbf{D}(p), p \rangle \ge c_d \|p\|^2.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Jacobian Preservation.}
Near-singularity conditions ($J \to 0^+$) trigger gradient-flow correction
fields ensuring
\begin{equation}
\dot{L}(p) \le -\eta\|p - p^*\|^2,
\end{equation}
thus preventing manifold inversion or topological tearing.

\paragraph{Lyapunov Negativity.}
Across all stress trajectories, the Lyapunov derivative remains strictly
negative:
\begin{equation}
\dot{L}(p) < 0,
\end{equation}
guaranteeing global asymptotic stability within $\mathcal{R}_{\text{safe}}$.

